  "conclusions": [
    "Character materialization/conversion/destruction is the largest separately identified CPU path: almost half of guest instructions and two thirds of allocated blocks.",
    "Temporary divmod pairs account for another third of allocated blocks. Their allocation alone consumes about one tenth of guest instructions, before tuple ownership and cleanup.",
    "Digit-list growth, comprehension-result growth and two slices account for most remaining requested heap bytes, but relatively few blocks.",
    "Signature inference is compile-time work; this gap is emitted representation and runtime work, including generic integer/ownership/error checks and eager materialization.",
    "Next targets are a semantic-preserving character-to-int consumer path, scalarized nonescaping divmod pairs, and then known-length allocation or safe elimination of consumed slice/comprehension buffers. These measurements do not establish any particular future speedup or Rust parity."
  ]
